    'tool.mcp_manage.about' => 'Installed MCP: list — the list with revisions; update — put a new revision next to the current one (the same steps, a trial start; the same set of tools switches at once, another waits for mcp_install approve); rollback — back to the previous revision; rebuild — build the dependencies of the current revision again by the same steps (for example after NODE_MODULE_VERSION — a module built for another Node); remove — delete the code of an install (not an entry of .mcp.json); remove_entry — take an entry out of the .mcp.json of a context (name, context: mine, member:<number>, project:<number>; without context — the file of this chat), the install stays; allow / disallow — allow an MCP to a context or take it back (context: member:<number>, project:<number>, owners).',
    'tool.mcp_manage.no_right' => 'A participant with the mcp right manages MCP.',
    'tool.mcp_install.about' => "Installing an MCP server into the sandbox (a participant with the mcp right). fetch — get the sources: url — the https address of a git repository, or path — a .tar.gz/.zip archive in your sandbox; the answer has the README and the files of dependencies — read them and make a plan. install — run the plan: steps (commands in the folder of the revision by a separate MCP user, no secrets, e.g. npm ci, npm run build) and entry — the entry for .mcp.json (command; args with the path to the code through \${INSTALL_DIR}; env with secret names {NAME}); then a trial start and the list of tools. approve — approve the shown set: classes=hints (danger classes as the server marks them) or irreversible (all irreversible); then the entry appears in .mcp.json of this context. A ready mercurius.mcp.json in the repository is a ready plan.",
