    'tool.http_request.podderzhivayutsya_tolko_adresa_http_i' => 'Only http and https addresses are supported.',
    'tool.http_request.secret_about' => 'Name of a secret in the vault (/secret) for a header: the code puts its value in, you never see it. Secrets also go into the body or a header value by the mark {{secret:name}} — e.g. a login form username={{secret:panel-user}}&password={{secret:panel-pass}} with Content-Type application/x-www-form-urlencoded (the value is encoded for a form or JSON by the Content-Type), and into the query of the address when a site takes its password there: url http://site/?password={{secret:site-pass}} (only after "?", never in the host name). A request with a secret goes through the owner\'s card; "always allow" is for the set of secrets and the host. Cookies a server gives (a session after a login) the code keeps itself for the project or participant and sends with the next requests to the same host; you never see them. The form token of a page (<meta name="csrf-token">) the code keeps itself and adds as the X-CSRF-Token header to the next change on the same host — do not copy it; copy any long value whole, never shortened with "…".',
    'tool.http_request.secret_header_about' => 'Header for the secret; by default Authorization: Bearer <secret>, another header gets the bare value (for example X-Api-Key).',
    'tool.http_request.secret_https_only' => 'A secret is sent only over https.',
    'tool.http_request.secret_in_host' => 'A secret goes only into the query of the address (after "?"), the body or a header — not into the host name or the path: the host must be the one the grant is bound to.',
    'tool.http_request.secret_redirect_stopped' => 'The server redirected the request to %s — the secret was not sent there and the redirect was not followed. If this address is needed, make a separate request to it: the owner will get a card for the new host.',
